WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.996 --> 00:00:03.984
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.410
It was pretty hard
to get people to join

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.840
Infinite a lot of people were
like, oh it's like too hard.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.190
Like I don't know
how to do that.

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.150
And I think, for me, I
study human centered design,

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.490
so I started being
really methodical

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.350
and using a lot of
those design practices

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:31.580
that I learned in class.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:34.190
So doing researchers, doing
some research like well

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:37.700
could be like the pain
points, as we say in design.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.320
Is it a problem
of representation?

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.150
People don't see people
like them doing that work.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.820
Is it other problems, like
do we need more mentorship

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:51.420
within Infinite to help people
get these projects through.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.850
Like what drew me to
collaborate in theater

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.190
is that you have no choice.

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.937
So I'm interested
in why you chose

00:00:57.937 --> 00:01:00.270
to collaborate in the visual
arts, which a lot of people

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:05.280
think is a very
individualistic medium.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.920
Allan and I, we're twins, and
ever since we were little,

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.185
we both always collaborated
on various art projects

00:01:12.185 --> 00:01:13.560
and it was always
something where

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.370
we felt as though if we worked
together on an art project,

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.700
we would be able to accomplish
a lot more and in less time.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.640
And also the ideas
that we would create,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.850
if we collaborate and jump
ideas between each other,

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:31.440
it would just be better, or more
innovative, or something more

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.510
creative than if only
one of us worked on it.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:38.100
So then when we came
to MIT, we really

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.630
looked for an art
community to kind

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:44.260
of replicate that experience.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:46.510
But when I started actually
working on my project,

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:48.460
I realized that it's not mine.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.580
When I tell somebody
about my project,

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.250
then it becomes our project.

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:56.750
Like I auditioned for Emerson.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.430
I auditioned for
vocal jazz ensemble.

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.010
And I would say like a
lot of my best friends

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:05.390
are people that I met
doing music stuff.

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.320
But if you find that one
goal, that one passion that

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:13.440
unites people around it and you
allow freedom of collaboration

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:17.660
and freedom of speech,
basically, freedom of merging

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.030
and moldings different
things together,

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:23.290
I think that is super beautiful.

